1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,067
August twelfth, the year two thousand. The Barents Sea.

2
00:00:03,067 --> 00:00:08,535
The pride of the Russian Navy is running a practice attack, with one hundred eighteen men on board.

3
00:00:09,666 --> 00:00:14,710
At half past eleven, Moscow time, seismic needles jump in three countries.

4
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:18,913
Something inside the Russian fleet's exercise area has just exploded.

5
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:23,367
Two minutes and ten seconds later, they jump again. Harder.

6
00:00:23,367 --> 00:00:27,034
The second blast is picked up in Canada, and in Alaska.

7
00:00:28,166 --> 00:00:31,109
The submarine they called unsinkable is on the bottom.

8
00:00:31,109 --> 00:00:36,995
And in her ninth compartment, twenty three men are still alive in the dark, waiting for a rescue.

9
00:00:38,133 --> 00:00:42,887
For the next nine days, Russia tells the world a story. Radio contact.

10
00:00:42,887 --> 00:00:48,007
Air being pumped down. A rescue closing in. Almost none of it is true.

11
00:00:49,133 --> 00:00:52,393
When it is over, Moscow will say the crew died instantly.

12
00:00:52,393 --> 00:00:56,839
Then divers will bring up a dead officer with two soaked pages in his pocket.

13
00:00:57,966 --> 00:01:02,590
Pages that prove the men lived for hours, and that the story was a lie.

14
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:05,672
This is the black box breakdown of the submarine Kursk.

15
00:01:06,799 --> 00:01:08,445
This is Black Box Breakdown.

16
00:01:09,566 --> 00:01:12,883
Russia said they died instantly. They didn't.

17
00:01:14,033 --> 00:01:16,880
Chapter one. The boat they called unsinkable.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,628
In the last years of the Cold War, the Soviet Union built a class of submarine for exactly one job: killing American aircraft carriers.

19
00:01:26,766 --> 00:01:28,804
Nato called the class Oscar two.

20
00:01:28,804 --> 00:01:35,595
Each one ran near silent and stretched one hundred fifty four meters — about two jumbo jets, nose to tail.

21
00:01:36,733 --> 00:01:42,421
Under the outer casing sat a second, thicker hull, and inside that, nine watertight compartments.

22
00:01:42,421 --> 00:01:47,350
Her designers claimed a boat like this could survive a direct torpedo hit.

23
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:49,246
The press called her unsinkable.

24
00:01:50,366 --> 00:01:53,929
This is her sister ship, Orel. Same class, same yard.

25
00:01:53,929 --> 00:02:03,192
The Kursk herself was welded together at Severodvinsk, commissioned in December nineteen ninety four, and named after the greatest tank battle of the Second World War.

26
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:13,224
Her main weapon was the Granit, a cruise missile the size of a small aircraft — twenty four of them, in angled tubes between the two hulls.

27
00:02:13,224 --> 00:02:16,846
This is a real launch, filmed aboard her sister ship Tomsk.

28
00:02:17,966 --> 00:02:25,183
In nineteen ninety nine, under Captain Gennady Lyachin, the Kursk shadowed the American Sixth Fleet in the Mediterranean during the Kosovo war.

29
00:02:25,183 --> 00:02:29,120
She came home celebrated as the best boat in the Northern Fleet.

30
00:02:30,266 --> 00:02:40,301
According to post-disaster reporting, her officers disabled the emergency rescue buoy on that same deployment — afraid it might pop loose and betray her position to the Americans.

31
00:02:40,301 --> 00:02:41,734
It was never re-armed.

32
00:02:42,866 --> 00:02:45,452
Chapter two. A navy running on empty.

33
00:02:46,599 --> 00:02:51,302
Her home was Vidyayevo, a crumbling garrison town above the Arctic Circle.

34
00:02:51,302 --> 00:02:56,787
By nineteen ninety eight, sailors on Russia's nuclear submarines were going months without pay.

35
00:02:57,933 --> 00:02:59,921
The fleet around her was dissolving.

36
00:02:59,921 --> 00:03:08,539
In the five years after the Soviet collapse, the navy lost roughly half its ships, and over a hundred nuclear submarines sat rotting at their moorings.

37
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:11,353
The rescue force went first.

38
00:03:11,353 --> 00:03:21,134
The dedicated rescue mother-submarines were scrapped in the nineties, and the navy's best deep-rescue vehicles had sat in a Saint Petersburg yard since ninety four, waiting for repair money.

39
00:03:22,266 --> 00:03:31,435
What remained were two aging Priz rescue capsules — this is the exact type — and a surface rescue ship that was really a twenty year old converted lumber carrier.

40
00:03:32,566 --> 00:03:38,843
And in her bow, the Kursk carried a bargain from another era: the Type sixty five torpedo.

41
00:03:38,843 --> 00:03:43,642
Ten point seven meters long, five tonnes, and driven by high test peroxide.

42
00:03:44,766 --> 00:03:53,687
The Royal Navy had abandoned peroxide torpedoes back in nineteen fifty five, after one blew up inside the submarine Sidon and killed thirteen men.

43
00:03:53,687 --> 00:03:57,775
Most Western navies followed. Russia kept them. The range was cheap.

44
00:03:58,899 --> 00:04:03,798
The practice torpedo aboard came from a batch of ten made in nineteen ninety.

45
00:04:03,798 --> 00:04:07,297
Six of the ten had been rejected, for faulty welding.

46
00:04:07,297 --> 00:04:10,445
Practice weapons, investigators later wrote, simply got less scrutiny.

47
00:04:11,566 --> 00:04:13,684
Her crew had never fired one.

48
00:04:13,684 --> 00:04:18,274
Their last live torpedo shot, three years earlier, used a simpler battery weapon.

49
00:04:18,274 --> 00:04:23,922
The investigation later found their peroxide training was never verified, and some training-log signatures were faked.

50
00:04:25,066 --> 00:04:31,423
Then, in August of two thousand, the Northern Fleet gathered for its biggest exercise since the Soviet Union fell.

51
00:04:31,423 --> 00:04:35,437
More than thirty warships — with the Kursk given the starring role.

52
00:04:36,566 --> 00:04:42,749
She sailed on August tenth — the twenty seventh birthday of Dmitri Kolesnikov, the officer who ran her turbines.

53
00:04:42,749 --> 00:04:47,955
At home, he left his dog tag, his crucifix, and a poem for his new wife.

54
00:04:49,099 --> 00:04:51,946
Chapter three. Two minutes and ten seconds.

55
00:04:53,066 --> 00:04:53,774
August twelfth.

56
00:04:53,774 --> 00:05:02,627
The fleet spreads across the Barents Sea, and the flagship — the giant battlecruiser Peter the Great — takes station as the Kursk's practice target.

57
00:05:03,766 --> 00:05:07,710
At eight fifty one that morning, the Kursk asks permission to fire.

58
00:05:07,710 --> 00:05:10,668
Fleet control answers with a single word. Dobro. Good.

59
00:05:10,668 --> 00:05:13,954
It is the last word anyone will ever send her.

60
00:05:15,099 --> 00:05:21,147
Investigators would conclude that inside torpedo tube four, peroxide was seeping through a faulty weld.

61
00:05:21,147 --> 00:05:30,015
On contact with metal it decomposes, expanding five thousand times over — an unstoppable chemistry her crew was never trained to fight.

62
00:05:31,133 --> 00:05:33,734
This is the actual seismic record.

63
00:05:33,734 --> 00:05:43,270
At eleven twenty nine, Moscow time, the torpedo's fuel explodes — magnitude one point five, roughly a hundred kilograms of high explosive.

64
00:05:43,270 --> 00:05:45,004
The Kursk keeps moving.

65
00:05:46,133 --> 00:05:48,346
Fire is now loose in her bow.

66
00:05:48,346 --> 00:05:55,302
For the next two minutes and ten seconds, the Kursk keeps driving forward, carrying that fire toward the rest of her torpedoes.

67
00:05:56,433 --> 00:05:57,997
Then the second trace.

68
00:05:57,997 --> 00:06:03,079
Magnitude three point five — an estimated five to seven warheads detonating together.

69
00:06:03,079 --> 00:06:08,162
It is picked up in Finnmark, in Spitsbergen, in Canada, and in Alaska.

70
00:06:09,299 --> 00:06:15,288
None of this rests on Russian data. It sits in an official Norwegian government report.

71
00:06:15,288 --> 00:06:20,876
First explosion, seven twenty nine and fifty seconds, Greenwich time, strength one point five.

72
00:06:20,876 --> 00:06:24,868
The second, quote, much larger explosion — three point five.

73
00:06:25,999 --> 00:06:31,731
Aboard Peter the Great, sonar operators hear the detonation, and the crew feels the hull shudder.

74
00:06:31,731 --> 00:06:36,030
They report it. The report is dismissed, and the exercise sails on.

75
00:06:37,166 --> 00:06:41,160
The blast has gutted the first four compartments and everyone inside them.

76
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:46,152
The Kursk plows bow-first into the clay, one hundred eight meters down, and goes still.

77
00:06:47,299 --> 00:06:49,802
One system works exactly as designed.

78
00:06:49,802 --> 00:06:53,973
Both nuclear reactors sense the catastrophe and shut themselves down.

79
00:06:53,973 --> 00:06:59,812
The engineers who built those reactors saved the Barents Sea. Nothing saved the bow.

80
00:07:00,933 --> 00:07:03,441
Chapter four. Twenty three men aft.

81
00:07:04,566 --> 00:07:08,391
In the stern, past the reactors, twenty three men are still alive.

82
00:07:08,391 --> 00:07:14,127
Survivors from three compartments grope through the dark to compartment nine — the one with the escape hatch.

83
00:07:15,266 --> 00:07:18,252
Above them: one hundred eight meters of near-freezing water.

84
00:07:18,252 --> 00:07:23,229
A free ascent from that depth, without surface support, is close to a death sentence.

85
00:07:23,229 --> 00:07:25,219
They choose to wait for rescue.

86
00:07:26,366 --> 00:07:27,817
The fleet doesn't know.

87
00:07:27,817 --> 00:07:32,534
The Kursk's firing window closes at half past one in the afternoon. Silence.

88
00:07:32,534 --> 00:07:36,162
And for hours, nobody treats the silence as an emergency.

89
00:07:37,299 --> 00:07:45,893
Not until eleven that night — close to twelve hours after the explosions — does the Northern Fleet finally declare an alert and stop the exercise.

90
00:07:47,033 --> 00:07:52,625
And by then, the men in compartment nine had already started writing down exactly what was happening to them.

91
00:07:52,625 --> 00:07:55,862
We know — because of what divers would find in October.

92
00:07:56,999 --> 00:07:59,350
Chapter five. What Moscow said.

93
00:08:00,499 --> 00:08:03,502
What happened next is best told side by side.

94
00:08:03,502 --> 00:08:09,172
What Moscow said out loud — against what the record now shows was true, hour by hour.

95
00:08:10,299 --> 00:08:13,604
Monday, two days later, the navy finally speaks.

96
00:08:13,604 --> 00:08:16,909
It says there were, quote, minor technical difficulties.

97
00:08:16,909 --> 00:08:21,454
It says the accident happened on Sunday. It happened on Saturday.

98
00:08:22,599 --> 00:08:26,229
The navy says it is in radio contact with the crew.

99
00:08:26,229 --> 00:08:28,868
There had been no radio contact since Dobro.

100
00:08:28,868 --> 00:08:31,507
Not one transmission, before or after the blasts.

101
00:08:32,633 --> 00:08:37,842
It says air is being pumped down to the survivors. Nothing had ever docked with the wreck.

102
00:08:37,842 --> 00:08:40,600
That night, the rescuers were still just filming it.

103
00:08:41,733 --> 00:08:46,132
It says rescuers can hear tapping. An S O S, spelling the word, water.

104
00:08:46,132 --> 00:08:50,844
Investigators would later conclude the recorded taps did not come from the Kursk at all.

105
00:08:50,844 --> 00:08:53,044
The families dispute that to this day.

106
00:08:54,166 --> 00:08:58,957
And the new president, three months into the job, stays on vacation in Sochi.

107
00:08:58,957 --> 00:09:04,432
State television shows him hosting a barbecue, roughly a day after his best submarine went silent.

108
00:09:05,566 --> 00:09:09,471
He would say of it later: I probably should have returned to Moscow.

109
00:09:09,471 --> 00:09:10,973
But nothing would have changed.

110
00:09:12,099 --> 00:09:20,772
Even the military's own newspaper printed a warning about peroxide torpedoes — and overnight, the piece was replaced with speculation about a foreign collision.

111
00:09:21,899 --> 00:09:24,407
Chapter six. The rescue that wasn't.

112
00:09:25,533 --> 00:09:27,478
The real rescue looked like this.

113
00:09:27,478 --> 00:09:33,315
On the second night, the capsule A S thirty four found the wreck — by crashing into it.

114
00:09:33,315 --> 00:09:34,937
The impact damaged the capsule.

115
00:09:36,066 --> 00:09:41,741
It tried again and again to lock onto the escape hatch, and failed every time.

116
00:09:41,741 --> 00:09:45,525
Batteries died mid-attempt. Crews stripped its sister capsule for parts.

117
00:09:45,525 --> 00:09:47,795
Storms shut everything down for hours.

118
00:09:48,933 --> 00:09:53,338
Moscow's explanations are preserved in the Norwegian report. Bad weather. A damaged hatch.

119
00:09:53,338 --> 00:09:56,049
And a sixty degree lean of the hull.

120
00:09:56,049 --> 00:10:01,132
When the wreck was finally surveyed, the lean measured about one and a half degrees.

121
00:10:02,266 --> 00:10:11,104
Meanwhile, Britain had already loaded this — the L R five rescue submersible — onto a ship, and Norway had deep saturation divers standing by.

122
00:10:11,104 --> 00:10:12,872
Six countries offered to help.

123
00:10:13,999 --> 00:10:15,132
Russia said no.

124
00:10:15,132 --> 00:10:21,548
Day after day, for five days, while the tapping story ran on television, the offers sat refused.

125
00:10:22,666 --> 00:10:25,196
On August seventeenth, Moscow finally said yes.

126
00:10:25,196 --> 00:10:30,616
This ship, the Normand Pioneer, was already steaming north with the L R five aboard.

127
00:10:30,616 --> 00:10:33,507
She arrived on the evening of the nineteenth.

128
00:10:34,633 --> 00:10:41,399
Norwegian divers went down from the Seaway Eagle on the twentieth — and reached the hatch Moscow had called too damaged to open.

129
00:10:42,533 --> 00:10:45,726
They opened it the next morning, with minimal difficulty.

130
00:10:45,726 --> 00:10:48,919
Behind it, compartment nine was flooded to the ceiling.

131
00:10:48,919 --> 00:10:53,531
Nine days after the explosions, the rescue was over before it ever began.

132
00:10:54,666 --> 00:11:04,227
That evening, the fleet commander, Admiral Popov, went on television, removed his cap, and asked the mothers of Russia to forgive him for not bringing back their boys.

133
00:11:05,366 --> 00:11:10,650
The British commander who brought the L R five chose his words carefully, years later.

134
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:16,285
Given permission immediately, quote, maybe we could have been attempting a rescue about three days earlier.

135
00:11:17,433 --> 00:11:19,784
Chapter seven. Vidyayevo.

136
00:11:20,933 --> 00:11:23,837
Through all of it, the families were here.

137
00:11:23,837 --> 00:11:31,460
Vidyayevo, the closed garrison town the Kursk called home — being told, day after day, that the men below were breathing.

138
00:11:32,599 --> 00:11:38,913
At a briefing, Nadezhda Tylik, the mother of a lieutenant on board, stood up screaming at the officials.

139
00:11:38,913 --> 00:11:43,123
On camera, a nurse injected her with a sedative, through her coat.

140
00:11:43,123 --> 00:11:44,876
She was carried out mid-sentence.

141
00:11:45,999 --> 00:11:48,197
Her husband said he had asked for it.

142
00:11:48,197 --> 00:11:51,494
Months later, she said that was a lie told to protect her.

143
00:11:51,494 --> 00:11:54,515
In her words: the injection was done to shut my mouth.

144
00:11:55,633 --> 00:11:58,841
On August twenty second, the president finally came north.

145
00:11:58,841 --> 00:12:00,980
These are the Kremlin's own photographs.

146
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:07,754
Inside the officers' club: four hundred people, a meeting that ran for hours, and a room full of fury.

147
00:12:08,899 --> 00:12:11,136
Mothers and widows screamed over him.

148
00:12:11,136 --> 00:12:17,101
Two journalists smuggled inside heard one question again and again: why have you murdered our lads?

149
00:12:18,233 --> 00:12:24,101
He promised each family ten years of an officer's salary, housing, and education for the children.

150
00:12:24,101 --> 00:12:27,402
The state paid. The questions, it never really answered.

151
00:12:28,533 --> 00:12:33,143
The next day, he answered questions on state television, calm and controlled.

152
00:12:33,143 --> 00:12:35,064
This is that actual interview.

153
00:12:36,199 --> 00:12:41,936
Two weeks later, on American television, Larry King asked him what happened to the submarine.

154
00:12:41,936 --> 00:12:45,760
He paused, gave a small smile, and answered: it sank.

155
00:12:46,899 --> 00:12:48,858
Chapter eight. Two pages.

156
00:12:49,999 --> 00:12:56,530
In late October, a joint Norwegian and Russian dive team went back down to the wreck — this time, to bring the men home.

157
00:12:57,666 --> 00:13:01,230
Warrant officer Sergei Shmygin was the first man inside.

158
00:13:01,230 --> 00:13:07,171
Among the first four bodies raised that day was a captain-lieutenant from the turbine room.

159
00:13:07,171 --> 00:13:09,943
In his pocket: two folded, water-soaked pages.

160
00:13:11,066 --> 00:13:17,636
The first entry is timed one fifteen in the afternoon, written in a steady hand while the emergency lights still worked.

161
00:13:17,636 --> 00:13:21,702
All personnel from sections six, seven and eight have moved to section nine.

162
00:13:21,702 --> 00:13:23,892
There are twenty three of us here.

163
00:13:25,033 --> 00:13:27,689
The last entry is scrawled blind, in the dark.

164
00:13:27,689 --> 00:13:31,527
It is too dark to write here, but I will try by feel.

165
00:13:31,527 --> 00:13:34,183
There is no chance — ten to twenty percent.

166
00:13:34,183 --> 00:13:36,840
Let's hope that at least someone will read this.

167
00:13:37,966 --> 00:13:44,235
Then, for everyone at home. Regards to everybody. No need to despair. And a signature.

168
00:13:44,235 --> 00:13:44,653
Kolesnikov.

169
00:13:45,799 --> 00:13:52,351
Remember what the deputy prime minister had told the world — that almost everyone died before the boat hit the bottom.

170
00:13:52,351 --> 00:13:56,718
The note proved twenty three men lived for hours. The official story was dead.

171
00:13:57,866 --> 00:13:59,862
Forensics filled in the rest.

172
00:13:59,862 --> 00:14:05,848
As the oxygen thinned, a chemical air-regeneration cartridge touched oily water and flash-burned the compartment.

173
00:14:05,848 --> 00:14:12,233
Carbon monoxide did the rest. The official finding: they lived six to eight hours at most.

174
00:14:13,366 --> 00:14:20,806
The families' lawyer spent years arguing the fleet's own logs said otherwise — knocking, recorded from the seafloor into the second day.

175
00:14:20,806 --> 00:14:25,879
A military court refused to reopen the case. He fled Russia in two thousand seven.

176
00:14:26,999 --> 00:14:31,427
A second note came up in November, its author never officially confirmed.

177
00:14:31,427 --> 00:14:36,962
It described the gas and the rising pressure. We cannot last more than a day.

178
00:14:36,962 --> 00:14:41,758
The men's emergency breathing gear, it noted, was stored forward. Forward was gone.

179
00:14:42,899 --> 00:14:48,148
Dmitri Kolesnikov was buried in Saint Petersburg. Before sailing, he had left Olga a poem.

180
00:14:48,148 --> 00:14:54,445
When the hour comes to die, I want time to whisper one thing. My darling, I love you.

181
00:14:55,566 --> 00:15:00,724
The navy never gave Olga the other side of his note — the part written only to her.

182
00:15:00,724 --> 00:15:04,735
Her words: there is something in that note they don't want me to see.

183
00:15:05,866 --> 00:15:07,825
Chapter nine. The verdict.

184
00:15:08,966 --> 00:15:12,710
A year later, Dutch salvage engineers did what many called impossible.

185
00:15:12,710 --> 00:15:20,199
Twenty six cables, gripped straight through holes cut in the hull, hauled nine thousand tonnes off the seabed in a single pull.

186
00:15:21,333 --> 00:15:24,457
They sawed off the shattered bow and left it on the bottom.

187
00:15:24,457 --> 00:15:29,144
The rest came up under the barge Giant four — the heaviest object ever recovered from that depth.

188
00:15:30,266 --> 00:15:36,808
In this dry dock at Roslyakovo, investigators finally walked her decks, and ninety four more men came home.

189
00:15:36,808 --> 00:15:41,532
In the end, one hundred fifteen of the one hundred eighteen were recovered.

190
00:15:41,532 --> 00:15:42,622
Three never were.

191
00:15:43,766 --> 00:15:47,900
In July two thousand two, the prosecutor general announced the cause.

192
00:15:47,900 --> 00:15:52,035
High test peroxide, through a faulty weld, in torpedo tube four.

193
00:15:52,035 --> 00:15:55,417
Exactly what the seismographs had said from day one.

194
00:15:56,566 --> 00:16:03,867
His own report described, quote, stunning breaches of discipline, shoddy, obsolete and poorly maintained equipment.

195
00:16:03,867 --> 00:16:11,168
Then the file was classified. One hundred thirty three volumes. The public got four pages.

196
00:16:12,299 --> 00:16:13,632
Criminal charges: none.

197
00:16:13,632 --> 00:16:20,737
In the prosecutor's words, those who designed the torpedo couldn't foresee the possibility of its explosion.

198
00:16:20,737 --> 00:16:25,621
Fourteen admirals and officers were removed — officially, not as punishment.

199
00:16:26,766 --> 00:16:32,039
The counter-story never died: a collision with a Nato submarine shadowing the exercise.

200
00:16:32,039 --> 00:16:34,878
One admiral called himself eighty percent certain.

201
00:16:34,878 --> 00:16:41,368
No evidence was ever produced — and the seismic record shows an explosion, not a collision.

202
00:16:42,499 --> 00:16:54,620
Twenty one years later, the same fleet commander repeated it on state media, saying he could name the Nato submarine with ninety percent probability — while admitting, quote, there is no evidence.

203
00:16:55,766 --> 00:16:57,804
Chapter ten. What the lie built.

204
00:16:58,933 --> 00:17:01,670
One more thing sank with the Kursk.

205
00:17:01,670 --> 00:17:07,926
Russia's biggest television channel, run by the oligarch Boris Berezovsky, had covered the disaster without mercy.

206
00:17:07,926 --> 00:17:11,054
The barbecue. The refused help. The sedated mother.

207
00:17:12,199 --> 00:17:13,729
The president was furious.

208
00:17:13,729 --> 00:17:21,760
Television people, he said, who had destroyed the very army and fleet, were now exploiting this misfortune to gain political capital.

209
00:17:22,899 --> 00:17:29,273
Within months, Berezovsky was pressured into selling the channel, and his star anchor was taken off the air.

210
00:17:29,273 --> 00:17:35,647
Channel One became, in effect, the state's — the first big domino in the takeover of Russian television.

211
00:17:36,766 --> 00:17:40,499
Twenty five years on, that is how the anniversaries frame this story.

212
00:17:40,499 --> 00:17:46,719
Not as an accident — but, in the words of the families' lawyer, as the place where the lies began.

213
00:17:47,866 --> 00:17:52,797
In two thousand nine, a scrap dealer near Murmansk noticed something in his yard.

214
00:17:52,797 --> 00:17:56,320
The Kursk's actual sail, cut from the wreck and forgotten.

215
00:17:56,320 --> 00:17:58,785
The city raised it as a memorial.

216
00:17:59,933 --> 00:18:03,394
It stands there now, above the names of her men.

217
00:18:03,394 --> 00:18:06,855
To the sailors, the plaque reads, who died in peacetime.

218
00:18:07,999 --> 00:18:11,010
Machines fail. That's physics, and you can engineer against it.

219
00:18:11,010 --> 00:18:20,041
But almost everything that happened after eleven twenty nine that morning was a choice — and twenty three men waited in the dark while those choices were made for them.

220
00:18:21,166 --> 00:18:24,388
The front of Kolesnikov's note has been read around the world.

221
00:18:24,388 --> 00:18:29,368
The other side — the part written only to his wife — has never been made public.

222
00:18:30,499 --> 00:18:39,806
If nine days of official lies got to you, wait until you see the plane that vanished with two hundred thirty nine people on board — and the search that is still empty handed.

223
00:18:39,806 --> 00:18:43,090
That breakdown is on the channel now. Subscribe to Black Box Breakdown.

